+
+# Tags on posts
+
+## Context
+
+The posts list is one long feed, so readers cannot find posts on a topic.
+Authors need a way to tag posts, and topic pages and a tag cloud will be
+built on the same data later, so the storage and page contract must be
+stable before those features start.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Table `tags` (`id`, `name` unique, case-sensitive) and pivot `post_tags`
+  (`post_id`, `tag_id`, composite primary key), one row per attached tag.
+  `Post.belongsToMany('tags')` reads them; `syncPostTags()` in
+  `app/Models/PostTag.ts` writes them, because the ORM has no attach/detach
+  helpers. Tags no post uses any more stay in `tags`.
+- The form field is `tags`: one comma-separated text input. Names are
+  trimmed, blanks dropped, duplicates collapsed (first occurrence wins),
+  then each name must be 1–30 characters and a post may carry at most 5.
+  A violation is a 422 on the `tags` field, like any other field, and the
+  route schema answers it before anything is written.
+- `PostResource` always emits `tags: string[]`, names sorted by code point,
+  `[]` for a post without tags. Every page that shows a post reads it.
+- `/posts?tag=<name>` filters the list; page links keep `tag`, an unknown
+  name lists nothing. The filter parameter is `tag`, never `tags`.
+- Post writes and tag syncs run in one `Post.transaction()`, and destroy
+  removes the pivot rows before the post.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Topic pages and a tag cloud can read `tags`/`post_tags` directly and rely
+  on the `tags: string[]` prop without touching the controller.
+- Renaming a tag or changing case-sensitivity is a schema decision, not a
+  form change: the unique index is case-sensitive by SQLite's default
+  collation.
+- Unused tags accumulate; a cleanup job is a separate decision.
